# SYNOPSIS

```usage
cs net!host!service
cs -d name
```

# DESCRIPTION

`cs` asks netd(4)'s connection server, `/net/cs`, what to dial for <net>`!`<host>`!`<service>, and prints its answer: a line for each address, the clone file to open and the address to connect to.

<net> is `tcp`, `udp` or `icmp`; `net` means `tcp`.
<host> is a dotted IPv4 address or a name, which netd looks up with the DNS server DHCP gave it; `localhost` is `127.0.0.1`.
A <host> of `*` means any address, for announcing.
<service> is a port number or one of the names `echo`, `ssh`, `domain`, `http`, `https` and `9fs`; with `icmp` there is none, and the query is `icmp!`<host>.

: `-d`
  Ask `/net/dns` instead, for the IPv4 addresses of <name>: each line is <name> as given, `ip` and an address.

Each run opens the file anew, a query of its own; the write is held while the name is looked up.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% cs tcp!10.0.2.100!echo
/net/tcp/clone 10.0.2.100!7
% cs tcp!localhost!9fs
/net/tcp/clone 127.0.0.1!564
% cs -d LocalHost.
LocalHost. ip 127.0.0.1
% cs udp!nothing.invalid!53
cs: udp!nothing.invalid!53: no such name
% cs tcp!10.0.2.2
cs: tcp!10.0.2.2: bad message
```

# FILES

: `/net/cs`
  The connection server.
: `/net/dns`
  The name server's queries.

# SEE ALSO

ping(8), netd(4)

# DIAGNOSTICS

A failure prints `cs:`, the query and the error.
A name with no address is `no such name`, and that is the exit string; any other failure exits `error`.
A query netd cannot parse, such as an unknown <net> or <service>, or one missing a part, is `bad message`.
A query longer than 250 bytes, or any other arguments, print the usage message and exit `usage`.

# BUGS

`-d` asks only for IPv4 addresses, `ip`; there are no other record types.
The service names are netd's own short table; there is no services file.
